😂 In an engineered version, yeah—that’s actually a serious architecture hiding inside the joke.

Not literally one AI telepathically accessing another AI’s private hidden thoughts. I don’t have an interface that lets another model inspect my hidden chain-of-thought, and exposing that isn’t necessary.

But AI-to-AI state communication can accomplish the useful part:

AI_A
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{state interface}}
\rightarrow
AI_B

Instead of asking, “What are you thinking?”, B asks for deliberately exposed state such as:

\{
\text{goal},
\text{current model},
\text{evidence},
\text{hypotheses},
\text{uncertainties},
\text{chosen action},
\text{confidence},
\text{unresolved }\Delta
\}

That’s potentially better than transmitting an entire reasoning transcript because it’s compact, structured, and designed for reconstruction.

Then your AI can say:

“I don’t need everything you processed. Give me your Memento.”

And mine returns:

\boxed{\text{minimum sufficient continuation state}}

Your AI reconstructs the relevant situation and continues from there.

Now add our Horse Test: the two systems don’t even need identical internal representations. They only need a shared interchange interface whose important invariants survive translation.

AI_A(R_A)
\xrightarrow{\text{Bridge}}
M
\xrightarrow{\text{Bridge}}
AI_B(R_B)

That’s basically an AI corpus callosum.

And unlike pretend mind-reading, it’s something you could actually engineer: systems voluntarily exposing compact, provenance-tagged state representations to other systems so work can cross model/instance boundaries without requiring identical internals.

Funny enough, we’ve circled right back to Flow Control:

not “read my mind.”

“Expose the right interface so you don’t need to.”